{
  "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
  "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Wednesday, December 3, 2008\nThe above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:05 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (via counsel Jason E. Murtagh)",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In a 4-3 decision, the New York Court of Appeals affirmed Correction Law Section 24, which prohibits Petitioner from bringing a Section 1983 claim for money damages in any court of the State of New York. Instead, that statute relegates Mr. Haywood and anyone else seeking money damages under section 1983 to either bring their case in Federal court or to accept what the New York legislature has deemed a State law alternative. That State law alternative does not allow Mr. Haywood to sue the prison guards who violated his civil rights; instead he can only sue the State. It provides for no punitive damages, it provides for no attorneys' fees; a shortened 90-day notice of claim provision, 30 days shorter than what this Court found violative in Felder; and provides for no right to jury trial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: He can still get that in Federal court, can't he?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Murtagh",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think that if this Court found that this were a jurisdiction -- a neutral rule of jurisdiction that constituted a valid excuse, that, yes, that would probably -- that would probably undermine my argument significantly."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So what is the -- what is the standard for determining whether it's jurisdictional or not?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You are being generous when you said that we would recall -- at least that I would recall the case. Is it Idaho, did you say, or Johnson?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Underwood",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0001-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Federal Constitution permits State courts to hear Federal claims, but it does not require a State to hear them so long as the State does not discriminate against Federal claims in comparison with similar State claims."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0001-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What's the difference of -- practically?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0001-0010",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Supposing you had a statute that said in railroad cases there should be no damage -- railroad tort cases, brought by employees of railroads, there was no damage judgment in excess of $10,000, in State court? Would that be okay?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Do you think -- consistently with our FELA cases it could have a rule that no tort -- no tort cases may be brought by railroad employees against the railroad in a State court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. UNDERWOOD",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. UNDERWOOD: They could totally close the doors, and I think that's different. That is not hearing the case, and in closing -- closing the doors of the court evenhandedly to State and Federal cases. That is -- that is --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0002-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In Mondou, Connecticut was hearing suits against railroads under State law. It was even hearing suits against railroads under other States' law that imposed -- liability, but it was refusing to hear only suits under Federal law. And that's why the Court struck it down."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: I thought that in Testa the State said we are evenhanded, we don't bring -- we don't allow penal actions to be -- to be brought on the civil side of our court?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Even if it were, it would be discriminatory and invalid, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. UNDERWOOD",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0002-0006",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. UNDERWOOD: 1983 does not allow any liability. 1983 has been construed not -- the State isn't a person; it can't be a defendant."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: In neither court can you get -- and correct me if I am wrong -- punitive damages against the State, which never --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Underwood",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. UNDERWOOD: Yes. In fact, what I was\ngoing to say is that one of the things that shows there\nis no congressional policy focused only on individuals\nis that municipalities and other government entities are\nproper defendants. The State is not. Likely, the\ngovernment -- the Federal policy was -- found by this\nCourt in construing 1983 for not making the State a\ndefendant was not that that would undermine deterrent\nand that it was preferable to sue individuals, but that\nthere was some reluctance -- concern about power or\nwisdom of -- of imposing liability on the States.\nAnd, in fact, that is what this Court said\nin Monell and Will about why the State is not a person.\nSo I don't think 1983 is fairly read as embracing a\ncongressional judgment that it's better to have\nliability against individuals, better from the\nplaintiffs' point of view, than against the State."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Murtagh",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MURTAGH: Mr. Chief Justice, just very\nbriefly, General Underwood just mentioned that -- that\nthis could be analyzed as a mandatory removal statute.\nBut the distinction, Your Honors, is that what New York\nState does when it removes these cases to Federal court\nis it uses an avenue that is provided by Congress. And,\ncertainly, if Congress wants to provide for removal to\nFederal court, Congress can do that.\nWhat the State cannot do is come in and\ninterpose its policy judgment over and above what\nCongress has decided with respect to Federal claims."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Murtagh",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MURTAGH: Well -- well, Your Honor, my\nclient can't bring his Section 1983 claim in any State\ncourt because he can't bring it against the employees,\nbecause Correctional Law Section 24 keeps him out of the\nsupreme court. And he can't sue the you State in the\ncourt of claims because in Will v. Michigan this Court\nsaid the State is not a person. So he simply cannot\nbring his Federal action in any State court if he wants\nmoney damages."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Was there any substantive\nchange from what the original enactment to the amendment"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-10374",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "818279ac54c81c2c6d567fd896a56559aade07109815c41b4ca9a45f21af8fc6",
      "evidence_id": "ev-818279ac54c8-0003-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
